# KEYMAP.md §Global (Normal mode, all Hub screens) — `j`/`k`, `gg`/`G`, `ctrl-d`/`ctrl-u`
#
# The `busy` preset publishes four worktrees across two repositories, which is the smallest
# list where "move one row", "jump to an end" and "jump half a page" have three different
# answers. The oracle is `focused`, which names the cursor's own row index, plus the row count
# the list is being moved through — a movement scenario that did not pin the list length would
# pass just as happily against a list of one.
fixture: busy
await idle 30000
assert screen == Hub && hub_pane == List && hub_tab == Worktrees
assert lists.worktrees.rows[3] exists && lists.worktrees.rows[4] absent
assert focused == worktrees.row[0]

# `j` and `k` move one row and stop at the ends rather than wrapping.
key j
await focused == worktrees.row[1]
key j
await focused == worktrees.row[2]
key k
await focused == worktrees.row[1]

# `G` is the last row; `gg` is the first. Both are two-keystroke gpui sequences.
key G
await focused == worktrees.row[3]
assert mode == Normal && key_contexts[0] == Hub && key_contexts[1] == Worktrees
shot last-row
dump last-row
key g g
await focused == worktrees.row[0]

# A half page of a four-row list is the rest of it, so `ctrl-d` lands on the last row and
# `ctrl-u` comes back to the first. Neither leaves the list or changes the pane.
key ctrl-d
await focused == worktrees.row[3]
key ctrl-u
await focused == worktrees.row[0]
assert hub_pane == List && overlay absent
dump settled
quit
